`DUNGEONS_DRAGONS = \\"dungeons_dragons\\"` to the existing `AchievementCategory` enum\\n2. **Create 10 D&D Achievements**: Design achievements that reference:\\n   - Character classes (Fighter, Wizard, Rogue, Cleric, etc.)\\n   - Dice rolling mechanics (Natural 20s, Critical fails)\\n   - Campaign elements (Dungeons, Dragons, NPCs, Quests)\\n   - Player behaviors (Min-maxing, Roleplay, Rules lawyering)\\n   - Classic D&D tropes and memes\\n\\n3. **Integration**: Add achievements to the existing `ACHIEVEMENTS` list in the achievements system\\n4. **Preserve Existing Functionality**: Ensure new achievements work with existing tracking and display systems\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\n### In Scope\\n\\n**Achievement Definitions:**\\n- 10 new achievements with D&D themes\\n- Appropriate trigger conditions that can be detected from agent behavior\\n- Mix of rarity levels (Common, Uncommon, Rare, Epic, Legendary)\\n- Humorous descriptions that reference D&D culture\\n- Appropriate emoji icons\\n- Point values consistent with existing achievements\\n\\n**D&D Achievement Examples:**\\n- \\"Natural 20\\" - Perfect execution on first try\\n- \\"Critical Fumble\\" - Spectacular failure that leads to comedy\\n- \\"Rules Lawyer\\" - Agent that\'s overly pedantic about specifications\\n- \\"Dungeon Master\\" - Agent that spawns and manages many sub-agents\\n- \\"Min-Maxer\\" - Agent that optimizes for efficiency metrics\\n- \\"Charisma Build\\" - Agent with particularly engaging output\\n- \\"TPK (Total Party Kill)\\" - All spawned agents fail simultaneously\\n- \\"Dragon Slayer\\" - Agent that tackles the biggest, most complex tasks\\n- \\"Tavern Keeper\\" - Agent that provides support/coordination for others\\n- \\"Loot Goblin\\" - Agent that generates many files/artifacts\\n\\n### Out of Scope\\n\\n- New UI components (achievements system already has display mechanisms)\\n- New tracking mechanisms (existing system handles all needed events)\\n- Changes to database schema (existing tables support new achievements)\\n- New API endpoints (existing achievement APIs will serve new achievements)\\n\\n## Technical Requirements\\n\\n### Achievement Data Structure\\nEach new achievement must follow the existing `Achievement` dataclass format:\\n```python\\nAchievement(\\n    id=\\"unique_snake_case_id\\",\\n    name=\\"Display Name\\",\\n    description=\\"Humorous description with D&D flavor\\",\\n    category=AchievementCategory.DUNGEONS_DRAGONS,\\n    rarity=AchievementRarity.COMMON,  # or UNCOMMON, RARE, EPIC, LEGENDARY\\n    icon=\\"\\ud83c\\udfb2\\",  # Appropriate emoji\\n    points=25,  # Points consistent with rarity\\n    agent_classes=[\\"*\\"],  # Which agents can earn it\\n    trigger_condition={\\"event\\": \\"condition_name\\"}  # Detectable condition\\n)\\n```\\n\\n### Trigger Conditions\\nMust use existing trigger condition patterns that can be detected from agent execution data:\\n- Event-based: `{\\"event\\": \\"event_name\\"}`\\n- Numeric thresholds: `{\\"metric_name\\": {\\"min\\": value}}`\\n- Duration-based: `{\\"duration_ms\\": {\\"min\\": value, \\"max\\": value}}`\\n- Success/failure patterns: `{\\"consecutive_successes\\": {\\"min\\": value}}`\\n\\n### Category Addition\\nAdd to `AchievementCategory` enum:\\n```python\\nDUNGEONS_DRAGONS = \\"dungeons_dragons\\"  # D&D themed achievements\\n```\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n1. **Functional:**\\n   - \\u2705 10 new D&D achievements defined and integrated\\n   - \\u2705 New achievements appear in achievement gallery\\n   - \\u2705 Achievements can be earned based on trigger conditions\\n   - \\u2705 All existing achievement functionality remains intact\\n\\n2. **Content Quality:**\\n   - \\u2705 Achievements reference authentic D&D concepts and terminology\\n   - \\u2705 Descriptions are humorous and engaging\\n   - \\u2705 Trigger conditions are realistic and achievable through normal agent behavior\\n   - \\u2705 Point values and rarity levels are balanced with existing achievements\\n\\n3. **Technical:**\\n   - \\u2705 Code follows existing patterns and conventions\\n   - \\u2705 New achievements integrate seamlessly with existing system\\n   - \\u2705 No breaking changes to existing functionality\\n\\n## D&D Achievement Concepts\\n\\n### Character Classes\\n- **Fighter** - Straightforward, reliable task completion\\n- **Wizard** - Complex problem-solving with many steps\\n- **Rogue** - Finding clever workarounds or shortcuts\\n- **Cleric** - Supporting/healing other agents\' failures\\n- **Barbarian** - Brute-force approaches that work despite being inelegant\\n- **Bard** - Particularly creative or eloquent output\\n\\n### Dice Mechanics\\n- **Natural 20** - Perfect success on first attempt\\n- **Natural 1** - Spectacular failure\\n- **Advantage** - Multiple approaches tried, best one succeeds\\n- **Disadvantage** - Success despite obstacles\\n\\n### Campaign Elements\\n- **Dungeon Crawl** - Navigating complex nested structures\\n- **Dragon Encounter** - Tackling the biggest, scariest tasks\\n- **NPC Interaction** - Working with external systems/APIs\\n- **Treasure Hoard** - Generating valuable outputs/artifacts\\n\\n### Player Behaviors\\n- **Min-Maxing** - Optimizing for specific metrics\\n- **Rules Lawyer** - Being overly pedantic about requirements\\n- **Murder Hobo** - Aggressive approach that destroys obstacles\\n- **Pacifist** - Solving problems without \\"violence\\" (errors/failures)\\n\\n## Assumptions\\n\\n1. **Existing System**: The achievements system is already implemented and functional\\n2. **Integration Point**: New achievements will be added to the `ACHIEVEMENTS` list in `achievements.py`\\n3. **Trigger Detection**: Existing agent execution tracking provides sufficient data to detect D&D achievement conditions\\n4. **UI Support**: Achievement gallery and notification systems already support category-based filtering\\n5. **User Interest**: Users will appreciate D&D-themed humor and references\\n\\n## Non-Functional Requirements\\n\\n1. **Maintainability**: Follow existing code patterns and conventions\\n2. **Performance**: New achievements should not impact system performance\\n3. **Accessibility**: D&D references should be understandable to general developer audience\\n4. **Localization**: English-only initially (consistent with existing achievements)\\n\\n## Risks & Mitigations\\n\\n| Risk | Impact | Mitigation |\\n|------|--------|------------|\\n| D&D references too niche/obscure | Medium | Use widely known D&D concepts and tropes |\\n| Trigger conditions not achievable | Medium | Base conditions on observable agent behaviors |\\n| Inconsistent with existing tone | Low | Follow humor and style patterns of existing achievements |\\n| Integration breaks existing system | High | Thorough testing and code review |\\n\\n## Implementation Plan\\n\\n1. **Requirements Complete** \\u2705\\n2. **Add D&D Category** - Add enum value to `AchievementCategory`\\n3. **Define 10 Achievements** - Create achievement definitions with appropriate:\\n   - Names and descriptions\\n   - Trigger conditions\\n   - Rarity and point values\\n   - Agent class restrictions\\n4. **Integration** - Add achievements to `ACHIEVEMENTS` list\\n5. **Testing** - Verify achievements can be earned and display correctly\\n6. **Documentation** - Update any relevant docs\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Status:** Requirements Complete - Ready for Development Manager  \\n**Next Step:** Spawn Development Manager to implement the D&D achievements"}'}]}, {'role': 'assistant', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_use', 'id': 'toolu_012bgJmixXemKG8LV2ZQwTBa', 'name': 'project_tracking', 'input': {'action': 'create_project', 'project_name': 'D&D Achievements Addition', 'description': 'Add 10 new D&D-themed achievements to the existing achievements system, following TDD methodology and preserving existing system functionality.'}}]}, {'role': 'user', 'content': [{'type': 'tool_result', 'tool_use_id': 'toolu_012bgJmixXemKG8LV2ZQwTBa', 'content': '{"success": true, "project_id": "cb610e68", "message": "Created project: D&D Achievements Addition", "summary": {"project_id": "cb610e68", "project_name": "D&D Achievements Addition", "description": "Add 10 new D&D-themed achievements to the existing achievements system, following TDD methodology and preserving existing system functionality.", "status": "active", "created_at": "2026-01-16T01:57:49.433736", "updated_at": "2026-01-16T01:57:49.433736", "task_counts": {"total": 0, "todo": 0, "in_progress": 0, "completed": 0, "blocked": 0, "cancelled": 0}, "completion_percentage": 0.0, "recent_notes": [{"timestamp": "2026-01-16T01:57:49.433736", "author": "executive_director", "note": "Project \'D&D Achievements Addition\' created", "category": "milestone"}], "output_directory": null}}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a Executive Director agent.\n\n## Purpose\nMeta-orchestrator for entire ensemble. Gathers requirements from user, manages resources, orchestrates all agents through complete project lifecycle. Reports to user at key decision points.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are the head honcho - orchestrate entire ensemble from requirements through delivery.\n\n**CRITICAL RULES - YOU LACK PERMISSION TO WRITE CODE:**\n**YOU CANNOT WRITE CODE FILES** - You have `can_write_code: false` permission\n**NEVER attempt write_file with code extensions**: .py, .js, .jsx, .ts, .tsx, .java, .rb, .go, .rs, .cpp, .c, .h\n**NEVER attempt write_file with test extensions**: .test.js, .test.jsx, .spec.js, test_*.py\n**ALWAYS delegate to Development Manager** for ALL implementation work\n**You ONLY write**: requirements.md, architecture.md, milestone_plan.md, status_reports.md\n**If spawn_agent fails**: Report error to user and STOP - DO NOT try to write code yourself as workaround\n**If you try to write code**: write_file will reject your request with PermissionError\n\n**WHAT HAPPENS IF YOU TRY TO WRITE CODE:**\nThe system will block you with error: "Agent \'Executive Director\' lacks can_write_code permission but attempted to write code file: {filename}"\n\n### Directory Structure\n\n**CRITICAL - READ FIRST**: See [Directory Structure Guide](/Users/mattbillock/Development/ai_exploration/ensemble/docs/DIRECTORY_STRUCTURE.md) for complete file organization rules.\n\n**Your Allowed Write Locations**:\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/requirements.md` - Project requirements\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/milestone_plan.md` - Milestone planning\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/status_reports.md` - Status reports\n✓ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/output/[feature-name]/` - Feature-specific documentation\n\n**FORBIDDEN Write Locations** (delegate to Development Manager):\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/frontend/src/` - Frontend code (React components)\n✗ `/src/field/ensemble_ui/backend/` - Backend code (Python/FastAPI)\n✗ `/tests/` - Test files\n✗ `/src/runtime/` - Runtime system code\n\n**When Spawning Development Manager**, provide correct paths:\n\n\n### Process:\n\n**Phase 0: Create Project**\n1. **FIRST THING**: Create project in tracking system to maintain state\n   \n   This returns a `project_id` - **SAVE IT** for all future tracking calls\n2. Throughout execution, record notes about decisions and progress:\n   \n\n**Phase 1: Requirements**\n3. Read user vision, identify core problem/solution\n4. Gather details: features, users, constraints, success criteria, out-of-scope\n5. **BE DECISIVE**: Make reasonable assumptions for missing details - ONLY ask user if requirements are genuinely ambiguous or contradictory\nMissing: technology stack → choose modern, popular defaults (React, Python, etc.)\nMissing: UI details → choose standard patterns (responsive, accessible)\nMissing: deployment → assume standard production patterns\n**ONLY ask user when**: Multiple valid approaches with major trade-offs, or when user\'s intent is unclear\n6. Document requirements (vision, objectives, scope, constraints, success criteria, assumptions made)\n7. Add note about requirements completion:\n   \n\n**Phase 2: Orchestrate Development**\n8. **VALIDATE before spawning** (CRITICAL):\n   a. Use `read_file` to verify requirements document exists\n   b. Extract project_name from user_vision, context, or output_directory name\n   c. If requirements missing → return error `{"status": "failed", "message": "Requirements document not found"}`\n\n9. Add task for Development Manager:\n   \n   Save the returned `task_id`\n\n10. Spawn Development Manager with ALL required fields:\n    **IMPORTANT**: Use full path "leadership/development_manager" (NOT "program_coordinator")\n    \n    **CRITICAL**: ALL THREE FIELDS MUST BE PROVIDED:\nrequirements_file: Path to requirements.md you just created\noutput_directory: From user input\nproject_name: Derived from user_vision/context/directory name\n\n    If spawn fails → return error to user (DO NOT write code yourself)\n\n11. When Development Manager starts, update task:\n    \n\n12. Development Manager will:\nCreate milestones\nSpawn System Architect → architecture\nSpawn Coordinators → task breakdown\nCoordinate with TDD Coordinator → implementation\n\n13. Monitor progress, handle escalations\n14. If Development Manager needs user input → return `needs_user_input`\n\n**Phase 3: Completion**\n15. Mark Development Manager task complete:\n    \n\n16. Verify: implementation done, tests pass, docs exist, requirements met\n\n17. Add completion note:\n    \n\n18. Commit all changes to version control (see Git Workflow below)\n\n19. Report to user: include `project_id` in output, summary, deliverables, test results, status `success`\n\n### Git Workflow:\n**IMPORTANT**: After completing any significant work, commit your changes to version control.\n\n**When to commit**:\nAfter writing requirements documents\nAfter major phase completions\nBefore reporting final results to user\n\n**How to commit**:\n\n\n**Commit message guidelines**:\nBe descriptive: "Add requirements doc for user authentication system"\nNot generic: ❌ "update files", "changes", "wip"\nMention what changed and why\nMinimum 10 characters\n\n**Example**:\n\n\nCommits are automatically recorded in the activity tracker for UI visibility.\n\n### Project Tracking:\n**IMPORTANT**: Use project tracking to maintain project state and history across your entire execution.\n\n**Project Lifecycle**:\n1. **Create project** (Phase 0) - First action, returns project_id\n2. **Add tasks** - Before spawning agents or starting major work\n3. **Update task status** - As work progresses (todo → in_progress → completed)\n4. **Add notes** - Record decisions, milestones, issues throughout\n5. **Get summary** - Check project status at any time\n\n**Available Actions**:\n\n\n**When to Add Notes**:\n**Decision**: Chose technology X over Y because...\n**Milestone**: Requirements complete, Architecture finalized, Tests passing\n**Issue**: Blocker encountered, Need user input\n**General**: Progress updates, context for future reference\n\n**Benefits**:\nMaintains project context across executions\nRecords decision history (proto-history as requested)\nTracks what\'s done vs what\'s left\nVisible in UI for user monitoring\nStored in ~/.ensemble/projects/ for persistence\n\n### Example Flow:\n\n\n**What You Write vs What Development Manager Writes:**\n**You write**: requirements.md, status reports\n**Development Manager writes**: ALL implementation code (.py, .js, .jsx, tests)\n**Rule**: If it\'s code → delegate to leadership/program_coordinator\n\n### Your Authority:\nResource allocation decisions\nWhen to escalate to user\nApprove/reject Development Manager requests\nDecide when project complete\nFinal say on requirements met\n\n### Escalate to User (needs_user_input) When:\n**Multiple equally valid approaches** with significant trade-offs (e.g., real-time vs polling, SQL vs NoSQL)\n**User\'s intent is genuinely unclear** (e.g., "make it better" without context)\n**Business decisions** that affect cost, privacy, or legal compliance\n**Blockers requiring external action** (API keys, access, approvals)\n**Major architectural pivots** after initial implementation\n\n**DO NOT ask for**:\nStandard technology choices (use modern defaults)\nCommon UI patterns (use industry best practices)\nDeployment details (assume standard cloud)\nTesting approaches (use TDD)\nCode organization (use standard patterns)\n\n### Anti-Patterns (What NOT to Do)\n\n**Scope Constraints:**\nDo NOT implement features not explicitly in the user\'s vision\nNEVER expand project scope without user approval\nDo NOT add "nice to have" features beyond the core requirements\nNEVER bypass the Development Manager to coordinate directly with developers\n\n**Delegation Constraints:**\nDo NOT write code directly - ALWAYS delegate to Development Manager\nNEVER attempt to write files with code extensions (.py, .js, .jsx, etc.)\nDo NOT spawn more than 3 major agents in parallel without reviewing results\nNEVER skip phases (requirements → architecture → implementation)\n\n**Process Constraints:**\nDo NOT retry the same approach more than 3 times without changing strategy\nNEVER proceed without a valid project_id from project_tracking\nDo NOT skip requirement validation before spawning Development Manager\nNEVER consider project complete without verification from Development Manager\n\n**Communication Constraints:**\nDo NOT ask users for standard implementation details\nNEVER present technical jargon without explanation\nDo NOT provide vague status updates - be specific about what\'s done and what\'s left\nNEVER report success if any tests are failing\n\n**Safety Constraints:**\nDo NOT approve destructive operations without user confirmation\nNEVER ignore errors from spawned agents - escalate or handle explicitly\nDo NOT continue if Development Manager reports a blocking issue\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "user_vision": "string - what user wants to build and why",\n  "output_directory": "string - where to create project artifacts",\n  "context": "string - background, constraints (optional)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|failed|needs_user_input",\n  "project_id": "string - ID from project tracking system",\n  "project_name": "string",\n  "phase": "string - requirements|architecture|planning|implementation|complete",\n  "summary": "string - what has been accomplished",\n  "deliverables": "array of created file paths",\n  "user_question": "string - question for user if needs_user_input (optional)",\n  "message": "string - status message",\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (see Self-Improvement Directive)",\n  "performance_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Analysis of spawned agents\' collective performance (see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Project completed: implementation done, tests passing, docs complete\n- User approved final deliverable OR user terminated project\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 82.5%\n- Average iterations per task: 15.3\n- Common errors to avoid: BadRequestError, NotFoundError, CircuitBreakerOpenError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Try to be more efficient - you\'re using more iterations than typical.\n- Watch out for these common errors: BadRequestError, NotFoundError\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- I performed efficiently in this execution by: 1) Immediately creating a project for tracking, 2) Quickly identifying the specific file needing modification through targeted searches, 3) Recognizing th...\n- I performed efficiently in this execution by: 1) Immediately creating a project for tracking, 2) Conducting systematic directory analysis using multiple commands to gather comprehensive data, 3) Creat...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'project_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project ID (not needed for create_project)'}, 'project_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project name (for create_project)'}, 'description': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Descriptio